[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Default and Static Methods in Interfaces (Core Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before Java 8, interfaces could only declare abstract methods. This meant that any class implementing the interface had to provide concrete implementations for all those methods. This rigid structure posed challenges when evolving interfaces, as adding a new method would break all existing implementations. Java 8 introduced default and static methods in interfaces to address these limitations, providing more flexibility and enabling interface evolution without disrupting existing code."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Default Methods"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Default methods allow you to add new methods to an interface without breaking existing implementations. They provide a default implementation that implementing classes can either use or override with their own specific implementation. Default methods are declared using the `default` keyword."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Syntax"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"public interface MyInterface {
    void abstractMethod();

    default void defaultMethod() {
        // Default implementation
        System.out.println("Default implementation of defaultMethod");
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Example"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"interface MyInterface {
    void display(String message);

    default void printMessage(String message) {
        System.out.println("Printing: " + message);
    }
}

class MyClass implements MyInterface {
    @Override
    public void display(String message) {
        System.out.println("Displaying: " + message);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass obj = new MyClass();
        obj.display("Hello, Display!");
        obj.printMessage("Hello, Print!"); // Uses the default implementation
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, `MyClass` implements `MyInterface`. It provides an implementation for the abstract method `display()`, but it uses the default implementation of `printMessage()` provided by the interface.  If `MyClass` needed a specific implementation for `printMessage()`, it could override it."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Overriding Default Methods"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"A class implementing an interface with a default method can override the default implementation. This allows the class to provide its own specific behavior."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"interface MyInterface {
    void display(String message);

    default void printMessage(String message) {
        System.out.println("Printing (Interface): " + message);
    }
}

class MyClass implements MyInterface {
    @Override
    public void display(String message) {
        System.out.println("Displaying: " + message);
    }

    @Override
    public void printMessage(String message) {
        System.out.println("Printing (Class): " + message);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass obj = new MyClass();
        obj.display("Hello, Display!");
        obj.printMessage("Hello, Print!"); // Uses the overridden implementation
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `MyClass` overrides the `printMessage()` method, providing its own implementation. When `obj.printMessage()` is called, the overridden implementation in `MyClass` is executed instead of the default implementation in `MyInterface`."},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Static Methods"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Static methods in interfaces are similar to static methods in classes. They are associated with the interface itself, not with any specific instance of a class implementing the interface. They can be called directly using the interface name."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Syntax"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"public interface MyInterface {
    void abstractMethod();

    static void staticMethod() {
        // Static implementation
        System.out.println("Static implementation of staticMethod");
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Example"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"interface MyInterface {
    static void utilityMethod(String message) {
        System.out.println("Utility: " + message);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyInterface.utilityMethod("Hello, Utility!"); // Calling the static method
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `utilityMethod()` is a static method in `MyInterface`. It's called directly using `MyInterface.utilityMethod()`. Static methods in interfaces are often used for utility functions that are related to the interface but don't belong to any specific implementation."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences Between Default and Static Methods"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default methods are instance methods, while static methods are associated with the interface itself."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default methods can be overridden by implementing classes, while static methods cannot be overridden."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Default methods are accessed through an instance of the implementing class, while static methods are accessed using the interface name."},{"order":25,"type":"heading","text":"Multiple Inheritance of Behavior"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Java does not support multiple inheritance of classes. However, with default methods, an interface can provide a default implementation, and a class can implement multiple interfaces, inheriting the default implementations from each. This allows a form of multiple inheritance of behavior."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Diamond Problem"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a class implements multiple interfaces that have default methods with the same signature, the 'diamond problem' can occur. The compiler will require the class to explicitly override the conflicting default method to specify which implementation to use, or to provide its own."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"interface InterfaceA {
    default void commonMethod() {
        System.out.println("InterfaceA's implementation");
    }
}

interface InterfaceB {
    default void commonMethod() {
        System.out.println("InterfaceB's implementation");
    }
}

class MyClass implements InterfaceA, InterfaceB {
    @Override
    public void commonMethod() {
        // Resolve the conflict by providing a specific implementation
        InterfaceA.super.commonMethod(); // Call InterfaceA's implementation
        InterfaceB.super.commonMethod(); // Call InterfaceB's implementation
        System.out.println("MyClass's combined implementation");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass obj = new MyClass();
        obj.commonMethod();
    }
}"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `MyClass` implements both `InterfaceA` and `InterfaceB`, both of which have a default method called `commonMethod()`. To resolve the conflict, `MyClass` overrides `commonMethod()` and explicitly calls the implementations from both interfaces using the `InterfaceName.super.methodName()` syntax. This allows the class to combine the behavior from both interfaces or provide its own entirely new implementation."},{"order":31,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"Default and static methods in interfaces provide powerful mechanisms for evolving interfaces and adding utility functionality without breaking existing code. They enable a limited form of multiple inheritance of behavior and offer greater flexibility in designing and maintaining Java APIs. Understanding how to use and resolve conflicts with default methods is crucial for effective interface design in modern Java development."}]
